Z||000003||ITB 2M4||12-22-2001||07:21 PM||||It could just be the way your front end alignment is.  My truck 'hops' the front tires in sharp turns as well, and any RWD car with ltd. slip will hop the tires when you turn the wheel in a slow sharp corner (try pushing that same car with the wheels turned, and you'll see what I mean).<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||10
Z||000004||Bruiser||12-23-2001||04:03 AM||||My car does the same thing. But also around big turns it seems to fade, like you'd enter the turn, and you'd have the angle set. And then you'd have to adjust it and turn the wheel more to keep the car going through at the same trajectory. But I don't know whether thats me just plowing cause the roads are kinda icy/wet or what.||||reg||6
Z||000005||Mickey_Moose||12-23-2001||12:17 PM||||17,000 is not alot of miles, but the age of the car is also a factor, especially one that is not driven - as parts tend to dry up and wear out alot faster (rubbers, gaskets, seals, bushings.<P>Having a car with little miles on it is nice - but it is also hard on the car (there is no constant movement of the parts to keep them lubricated).<P>just my 2 cents<BR>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
